## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Walk in the Neighborhood</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is My Favorite.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Interpret simple school notices. communication: Facilitate a simple discussion among classmates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret simple school notices using gestures to indicate main ideas (e.g., 'The notice says there's a park trip.' and points to a park picture). Can help facilitate a discussion on favorite places in the neighborhood by asking simple questions and using visual aids (e.g., 'What do you like at the park?' while showing a picture of a playground or pointing to a specific part of the map). Can guide classmates in expressing opinions about neighborhood places by using gestures (e.g., points to school and says, 'This school is the best.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and starts a quick "Show and Tell" review. Students stand up, show their "Favorite Place Poster" (from the 21st Century Skills Project, which they've been working on) to a classmate, and briefly say, "This is my favorite [place] because it is [adjective]." (e.g., "This is my favorite park because it is fun and big.") The teacher encourages students to use gestures to point to parts of their poster. Focus on pronunciation of /f/ in 'favorite' and /b/ in 'best' or 'big'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a simple school notice with a picture of a park. "Look! This is a notice from our school. It says, 'Neighborhood Fun Day at Green Park! Come play on Saturday. It has the best playground!'" The teacher points to the park and the playground on the notice. "I see a park and a playground. This notice is about a fun day at the park. I can help my friends understand this notice." The teacher models interpreting and explaining the notice simply, using gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is this paper about?" (Expected: A park day / Fun day / Neighborhood.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the best thing about Green Park, according to the notice?" (Expected: The best playground.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents 2-3 more simple, illustrated school notices or community flyers related to neighborhood places. These could be about:
                    <br>1. A library story time (e.g., "Our school library is the best place to read!").
                    <br>2. A street clean-up day (e.g., "Let's clean our street. It will be the most beautiful street!").
                    <br>3. A new playground opening (e.g., "Come see the biggest playground in our neighborhood!").
                    <br>Each notice should have a clear image and 1-2 simple sentences using target grammar and vocabulary. Genre: Simple public notices, Register: Informal, Format: Short text with visuals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediation: Interpret simple school notices. Facilitate a simple discussion among classmates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows each notice and asks students to point to the main picture and say what it is (e.g., "This is a library," "This is a street.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match the notice to a simple title the teacher provides (e.g., "Library Fun," "Clean Street Day," "New Playground").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks simple questions for each notice: "What is this notice about?" "What is the best thing about [place]?" (e.g., "What is the best thing about the library?")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice interpreting simple notices and explaining them to others using superlatives, simple present with 'because', and question structures. They use vocabulary like `neighborhood`, `park`, `place`, `playground`, `best`, `favorite`, `fun`, `big`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Guided Notice Interpretation:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with 2 new, simple school notices (similar to those presented, e.g., one about a community garden event "See the most beautiful flowers!" and another about a pet adoption fair "The friendliest pets!"). Students work individually to circle key words (e.g., `flowers`, `pets`, `beautiful`, `friendly`) and draw a small picture representing the main idea for each notice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Peer Explanation:</b> In pairs, students take turns explaining one notice to their partner. Student A says, "This notice is about a [community garden]. It says to see the most beautiful flowers." (points to picture). Student B then asks, "What is the notice about?" or "What is the best thing about it?" Student A responds, "It's about a garden, and it has the most beautiful flowers." Teacher circulates, encouraging use of gestures and correct pronunciation of /b/ and /f/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Facilitating Discussion Starters:</b> The teacher models, "My favorite place in the neighborhood is the park because it has the best playground." Students then practice asking each other, "What is your favorite place in the neighborhood?" and answering, "I like the [school/street/building] because it is [fun/big/safe/quiet]." Teacher prompts them to use gestures and point to pictures if they have them.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative group presentation and discussion. Students will use their "Favorite Place Poster" (Project 1). The mediation focus here is on explaining their poster, interpreting classmates' questions, and facilitating a short discussion about favorite places.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form small groups (3-4 students). Each student brings their "Favorite Place Poster."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One by one, each student presents their poster to their group. They clearly state, "This is my favorite [neighborhood place], the [park/school/street/building]." They then explain why it's their favorite using 'because' and superlatives, e.g., "I like it because it is the best place to play," or "It has the biggest trees." They use gestures to point to elements on their poster.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After each presentation, the presenting student facilitates a brief Q&A session. They can ask, "What do you think of my favorite place?" or "What is your favorite part of a park?" Group members ask simple questions like, "What is your favorite color on the poster?" or "Is it a big park?" The presenter interprets these questions and responds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The group then has a short discussion, guided by the student presenters, about their favorite neighborhood places, comparing them. (e.g., "My park is the best. Is your school the best?"). Teacher circulates, prompts for 'because', and supports mediation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Favorite Place Poster" and facilitate a short discussion within their small group, demonstrating their ability to interpret and explain their work and guide classmates' opinions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interprets Poster:</b> Student clearly explains their poster using gestures to indicate main ideas (e.g., 'This park has the best playground' and points to it).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Facilitates Discussion:</b> Student asks simple questions and uses visual aids (poster) to help discussion (e.g., 'What do you like at my park?').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Expresses Opinions:</b> Student uses target grammar (superlatives: 'best', 'coolest'; simple present + 'because': 'I like this because it is fun').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of /b/ (best, big) and /f/ (favorite, fun).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Student actively participates and listens to classmates.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can explain my favorite place to my friends. Yes / No"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can use 'because' to say why. Yes / No"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the easiest part of explaining your poster to your friends?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult about explaining or asking questions about your friends' posters?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students mediate information and facilitate discussions? (e.g., gestures, visual aids, structured sentence frames).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for future lessons, especially for students struggling with explaining opinions or interpreting simple texts for others? (e.g., more sentence starters, pre-teaching specific question words).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "This Is My Favorite." theme. The next theme will introduce new vocabulary and scenarios, building on the communicative skills developed, possibly starting with listening comprehension in a new context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>